/*****************************************************************************
    jitXnot -- NEGATION OF A ZERO-VALUED FIELD, INTERPRETED ONLY. 2026-08-08.

    ⚠ THIS FIXTURE TOUCHES NO JIT. Its first version did, and asserted a JIT
    finding that was really a fixture defect: `if !xnIn;` with xnIn = 0 did not
    fire, and THE INTERPRETED ORACLE DID NOT FIRE EITHER. A divergence needs
    two engines disagreeing; both agreed here, so there was no JIT finding and
    the honest move is to take the JIT back out and ask the language question
    on its own.

    THE QUESTION: does `!` on a field CARRYING NUMERIC ZERO test true?

    Not idle. KANT-35 records `if !a;` as the correct spelling of a refusal
    guard, but every measured case there is an ABSENT ATTRIBUTE. A field
    holding the number 0 is a different animal, and bear-trap #26 is the
    standing reminder that presence and value are different questions on a
    GroupItem. Row 2 is the control: an absent field, KANT-35's own shape.

        row 1  xnIn = 0 (present, value zero)   `if !xnIn;`
        row 2  xnMissing (never defined)        `if !xnMissing;`
        row 3  xnIn = 1                         `if !xnIn;`   must NOT fire

    Row 3 is the anti-vacuity sibling: without it, a `!` that never fires would
    look like a correct answer on rows 1 and 2.
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;

register(JitXnot);
define
    xnIn;
    xnOut;
    xnProbe code={
        xnOut = 0;
        if !xnIn;
            xnOut = 1;
        };
    ;

xnIn = 0; xnOut = 9;
xnProbe();
print "XN row 1: xnIn = 0 (present, zero) -> xnOut =" xnOut " (1 = ! fired)":;

xnIn = 1; xnOut = 9;
xnProbe();
print "XN row 3: xnIn = 1                 -> xnOut =" xnOut " (0 = ! correctly did NOT fire)":;

print "XN SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
